A||||11||Pretender||front brakes really acting up!?!?!?!||||9||
Z||000000||Pretender||03-28-2000||01:45 AM||||My front brakes on my 88gt seem to be losing there power? it seems strange but i just put brand new crossdrilled rotors and carbo-mat. pads on them 4 months ago. I'm getting the clanking/krinking sounds all over again, i think they were always there but just "quieter". Well it's loud now! Sometimes when ever i stomp on the brakes my car seems like it's never going to stop, i keep on going even though the brake is flored. What could this be? i know that i could use a new set of pads in the rear but there not that bad. I remember reading a post awhile ago that everyones brakes krink and clank alittle bit but there is something wrong here. It's almost embarrising at a stop sign when all you can here is the what seems to be the sound of the calipers completly killing my brakes...can someone please help?<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||batboy||03-28-2000||01:55 AM||||Carbon-met pads are pretty slick until they warm up. Do they work ok when they're hot?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pretender||03-28-2000||02:20 AM||||when there hot they do seem to stop alot faster but there is still the loud krinking and clanking noise. When ever the car is first started up that is when the car doesn't seem to want to stop. even if i pump the brakes but the noise is throughout the warm-cold brake use. and it's staring to piss me off. All the money i'm putting into this thing....sometimes i wanna buy another conquest..and park this thing for another 2 years...*sigh*||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spidy||03-28-2000||02:51 AM||||I found that the krinking, canking and poor stopping was comming from my front calipers. After replacing them the ability to stop improved and the noise went away.<p>[This message has been edited by Spidy (edited 03-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||JetroGT||03-28-2000||05:53 AM||||Try semi-metalic pads. If your not constantly racing they will work great.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||03-28-2000||01:18 PM||||I installed the CarboMet pads on my Formula. When they're cold it requires a LOT of pressure to stop quickly. Locking the wheels is out of the question. Scary on cold mornings. Thinking about replacing them with semi-metallics. If your pedal actually goes to the floor, there is something elso wrong, too. Maybe air in the lines or bad master.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lawrence||03-28-2000||01:39 PM||||You have three problems with your brakes.  First, it's a Fiero.  They have lousy brakes. <P>Second, you are using CarboMets.  I have them on my car, and I don't like them.  When they are cold, they don't work worth a shit.  I don't understand why they need to "warm up" to work, but that seems counter productive.  We all want vented disks to keep things cool.<P>Third, your creaking is coming from one of two places.  First, you need to clean and lube the slider pins with a high viscosity, high temperature grease.  I've tried it all, and the best seems to be Dow Corning O-Ring grease.  I've tried the plain silicone grease (clear stuff that looks like Petroleum Jelly) and that works for a while, but it isn't as good as the Dow I've been using.  I've also used Anti-Sieze compound, and that works pretty well.<P>The other possible cause of the creaking is a delaminating pad.  My driver's rear started creaking, so I did the pin lube as above and it didn't go away.  I fought and fought until I replaced the pads -- creaking gone.  I mounted the pad in a bench vice and I could flex the backing plate and see the pad move a bit.  Very small, but it did move.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A> - WebElectric Magazine<p>[This message has been edited by lawrence (edited 03-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pretender||03-28-2000||02:46 PM||||"Clean and lube the slider pins"<P>this is a first time i've ever heard of slider pins? where are they located? is there a pic someone can email me or post? I'll probably end up replacing the calipers and master cyl. is there a caliper bolt on upgrade? maybe some-other GM car has the same bolt up pattern with a higher quality caliper? ||||reg||5
Z||000008||Raydar||03-28-2000||04:05 PM||||On an 88, the sliders are actually brass-looking sleeves, about 3/4" across, that the caliper retaining bolts go through. NOT to be confused with the bridge bolts, which are on the back "corners" of the caliper. The retaining bolts are further down towards the hub, and have Torx heads. Once you have the Torx bolts out, and the caliper off, you will be able to see a rubber seal on the inside and outside of each slider. The lube goes on the surface of the slider where it slides through the caliper. The seals keep it all inside. It's a whole lot easier to understand than it is for me to describe.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Fairly straight-forward, once you have it apart. <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 03-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Spidy||03-29-2000||12:19 AM||||Thanks Raydar, <BR>I had heard that the slider pins could be relubed. I just wasn't sure how to go about doing it. The rebuilds have worked well, as for the next time its the Dow for me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Spidy||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||03-29-2000||01:05 PM||||I have no experience with the 88 Fiero brakes, but on the non-88 calipers, all have two slider pins/hold down bolts combination. The caliper is a single piston floating design, so the caliper has to slide. Whenever you remove a caliper, these slider pins should be relubed with special grease that is available at all parts stores, like was mentioned earlier. Make sure the slider sleeve moves freely and doesn't stick. What was not mentioned is that the slider rubber O rings should be replaced too (there are two O rings mounted in the caliper slider holes per each slider).||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||03-29-2000||02:10 PM||||Batboy, The 88 slider doesn't use O-rings. The caliper rides directly on the slider. At least mine do, now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>(There's always the possibility that I missed something because I had my head in a "dark place". I'm known for that.)||||reg||11
